1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:03,773
I started vibecoding on February fourteenth, twenty twenty-six.

2
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:06,177
AI helped me turn ideas into software.

3
00:00:06,177 --> 00:00:07,767
My GitHub graph turned green.

4
00:00:07,767 --> 00:00:11,215
Then I noticed a problem: the agents could keep building, but too much of

5
00:00:11,215 --> 00:00:12,801
the work still came back to me.

6
00:00:12,817 --> 00:00:15,657
I'm Mark Hinkle, co-founder of All Things AI.

7
00:00:15,673 --> 00:00:19,667
I've been executive director of the Node.js Foundation, VP of developer

8
00:00:19,667 --> 00:00:24,109
relations at Cloud.com, and VP of marketing at the Linux Foundation.

9
00:00:24,334 --> 00:00:26,953
John Willis co-wrote The DevOps Handbook.

10
00:00:26,981 --> 00:00:31,114
He led training and services at Opscode, now Chef, worked at Enstratius,

11
00:00:31,114 --> 00:00:35,045
acquired by Dell, and co-founded SocketPlane, acquired by Docker.

12
00:00:35,386 --> 00:00:39,376
We know process, but lately we've spent more time managing than coding.

13
00:00:39,555 --> 00:00:42,086
Today, the old guys are dusting off their keyboards.

14
00:00:42,086 --> 00:00:45,696
I'm explaining the same context, handing work between agents, checking

15
00:00:45,696 --> 00:00:47,991
results, and rescuing failed attempts.

16
00:00:48,332 --> 00:00:51,009
More code means more decisions waiting for me.

17
00:00:51,292 --> 00:00:54,601
The question is how to improve the system so the human doesn't become the

18
00:00:54,601 --> 00:00:55,398
bottleneck.

19
00:00:55,994 --> 00:00:57,720
Mini Gibson is our experiment.

20
00:00:57,794 --> 00:01:02,357
We want a small model to learn concrete handoffs, responses to failed tests,

21
00:01:02,357 --> 00:01:04,047
and evidence of what changed.

22
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:08,429
We'll compare the base model, the model inside Gibson, and the tuned model

23
00:01:08,429 --> 00:01:13,259
inside Gibson. Our hypothesis is fewer requests for human help while the

24
00:01:13,259 --> 00:01:15,309
work meets the same quality bar.

25
00:01:15,813 --> 00:01:17,480
We now have two tracks.

26
00:01:17,496 --> 00:01:22,175
Our seven B model completed twenty training steps on Brev, using one H one

27
00:01:22,175 --> 00:01:27,423
hundred and NeMo Automodel. vLLM serves that adapter and passed connection

28
00:01:27,423 --> 00:01:31,347
checks. Separately, thirty B Lightning has a loaded adapter and development

29
00:01:31,347 --> 00:01:34,237
results, tested with Transformers and PEFT.

30
00:01:34,237 --> 00:01:36,583
We keep their datasets and results separate.

31
00:01:36,583 --> 00:01:41,169
The Gibson is our process framework: define the task, isolate changes, run

32
00:01:41,169 --> 00:01:43,351
checks, and get independent review.

33
00:01:43,351 --> 00:01:47,031
Gibson Environment is the foundation that runs tools and checks their

34
00:01:47,031 --> 00:01:49,736
results. Connecting it to this model is still ahead.

35
00:01:49,736 --> 00:01:54,218
The model proposes repairs. Independent review and our approval rules stay

36
00:01:54,218 --> 00:01:54,949
in place.

37
00:01:54,949 --> 00:01:57,724
This is our existing Agent Kontrol dashboard.

38
00:01:57,724 --> 00:02:02,566
It shows Claude, Grok, and Codex across the fleet: working, finished, or

39
00:02:02,566 --> 00:02:05,963
waiting for me. We can see where work gets stuck.

40
00:02:06,246 --> 00:02:10,275
The experiment asks whether a specialized model can reduce the work that

41
00:02:10,275 --> 00:02:11,362
needs our attention.

42
00:02:11,703 --> 00:02:15,859
For the seven B rehearsal, we started with two hundred eighty-three source

43
00:02:15,859 --> 00:02:20,201
records. Filtering and tokenization left one hundred eighty-eight training

44
00:02:20,201 --> 00:02:24,945
rows and thirty development rows, at a two thousand forty-eight token limit.

45
00:02:25,449 --> 00:02:28,916
We saved and reloaded a small LoRA adapter.

46
00:02:29,629 --> 00:02:33,437
The separate OpenHands track still needs its training-data record matched to

47
00:02:33,437 --> 00:02:34,965
the evaluated adapter.

48
00:02:35,561 --> 00:02:38,332
Working connections did not mean working software.

49
00:02:39,044 --> 00:02:43,097
The seven B adapter passed a required tool call, but failed a real

50
00:02:43,097 --> 00:02:47,973
repository change. Automatic mode returned prose; forced calls repeatedly

51
00:02:47,973 --> 00:02:50,794
targeted the wrong file. No edit was applied.

52
00:02:50,794 --> 00:02:55,159
In the separate thirty B development run, the base passed six of twenty-four

53
00:02:55,159 --> 00:02:57,825
tasks; the adapter passed twelve.

54
00:02:57,841 --> 00:03:00,082
Those are exploratory development results.

55
00:03:00,082 --> 00:03:04,041
We still need to match the training records, lock the comparison, and test

56
00:03:04,041 --> 00:03:08,599
on untouched tasks. Better quality and fewer human interruptions are still

57
00:03:08,616 --> 00:03:09,417
unproven.

58
00:03:09,417 --> 00:03:11,398
Three optional pieces could help.

59
00:03:11,414 --> 00:03:14,200
Nemo Gym would organize repeatable trials.

60
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:18,004
Jev would turn locked results into advice for John and me to review.

61
00:03:18,508 --> 00:03:22,543
Langfuse would show the sequence of model calls, tools, and checks.

62
00:03:22,954 --> 00:03:26,217
Those connections are proposed; we're not claiming they're running.

63
00:03:26,217 --> 00:03:30,141
We want useful work that passes checks and independent review, with less

64
00:03:30,141 --> 00:03:31,848
chasing and rescuing from us.

65
00:03:31,848 --> 00:03:34,699
That's how we keep the human from becoming the bottleneck.

66
00:03:34,715 --> 00:03:36,778
Follow the repository on screen.
